import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { url } from '../../../utils/url';
---
<BaseLayout
  lang="en"
  translated
  title="AI transparency — tools, safeguards and limits"
  description="Which AI tools were used to develop and document LIVEX, in which phases and with what safeguards — and what the project is not."
>
  <PageHeader
    lang="en"
    index="21"
    eyebrow="About · AI transparency"
    title="Say what the AI did, and what it did not do."
    lead="Two distinct uses of the word “AI” coexist in LIVEX: the assistance of generative tools during development, and the behaviour of the entities of the simulated world. This page tells them apart."
    crumbs={[{ label: 'About', href: 'a-propos/' }, { label: 'AI transparency' }]}
  />

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>21.1</b> Principle</div>
      <h2>A solo project, augmented and responsible.</h2>
      <p>
        LIVEX is carried by a single person. It would have been <strong>hardly achievable alone</strong> without the
        help of artificial-intelligence tools, which speed up documentation, verification and certain repetitive tasks.
        This is a fact, and concealing it would be a form of dishonesty.
      </p>
      <p>
        This page describes <strong>where</strong> these tools intervene, <strong>how</strong> their output is
        controlled, and <strong>what they are not</strong> in the project.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>21.2</b> Tools and frameworks of use</div>
      <h2>Guarded assistance, never blind.</h2>
      <p class="mu">
        Assistance mostly concerns written output and verification. It decides nothing on its own: every phase below
        remains under the author's approval.
      </p>
      <div class="ov">
        <table class="tb">
          <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Use of AI</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Verification</td><td>Review and consistency checks on passages already written.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rewriting</td><td>Help with clarity and concision, subject to the author's approval.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Documentation</td><td>Correction and formatting of repository documents.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Code</td><td>A few functions, reviewed and tested like any other contribution.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Runs and calibration</td><td>Help preparing and analysing simulation runs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Review</td><td>Double-checking of proposals before integration.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>21.3</b> Written by hand, then verified</div>
      <h2>The decision remains human.</h2>
      <p>
        Design choices, architecture trade-offs and sensitive wordings remain written by the author, then verified.
        Nothing is published on the sole basis of an automatic generation: any claim about a capability, a figure or a
        property is confronted with the real state of the project before being released.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>21.4</b> Safeguards</div>
      <h2>What bounds the assistance.</h2>
      <p class="mu">
        Four structural controls keep assisted output from turning into an unverified claim.
      </p>
      <div class="g2">
        <div class="pl br" style="padding:18px"><h3>Human review</h3><p class="mu" style="margin:0">Any assisted output goes through a human review before being kept.</p></div>
        <div class="pl br" style="padding:18px"><h3>Tests</h3><p class="mu" style="margin:0">Assisted code is submitted to the same test suites as the rest of the project; no preferential treatment.</p></div>
        <div class="pl br" style="padding:18px"><h3>Traceability</h3><p class="mu" style="margin:0">The repository, its commits and its changelog remain the verifiable record of the work.</p></div>
        <div class="pl br" style="padding:18px"><h3>Register of claims</h3><p class="mu" style="margin:0">Sensitive claims on the site carry a dated internal source, regardless of how they were written.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>21.5</b> What the project is not</div>
      <h2>The agents are not language models.</h2>
      <div class="cc">
        <p>
          The entities of LIVEX <strong>are not</strong> language models, and the world is not “learned” by an AI. Their
          cognition rests on explicit, deterministic and inspectable rules. No generative model intervenes at the heart
          of the simulation: the question LIVEX asks — what local, replayable laws produce — requires a cognition that
          is poor but <em>transparent</em>, not an opaque network.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="mu" style="font-size:0.78rem">
        See also the distinction between simulated agent, learning AI and scripted character in
        <a href={url('comprendre/emergence/', 'en')}>Emergence explained</a>.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:18px">
        <a class="bt" href={url('a-propos/', 'en')}>← About</a>
        <span style="display:inline-block;width:8px"></span>
        <a class="bt" href={url('comprendre/limites/', 'en')}>Limits and scope</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
